    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "If we can decompose $ h(x) $ into the product of a function, $ f(x) $, and a probability density function, $ p(x) $, defined over the interval $ (a, b) $, then $$ \\int_{a}^{b}h(x)dx=\\int_{a}^{b}f(x)p(x)dx=E_{p(x)}[f(x)] $$ which means that the integral can be expressed as the expectation of $ f(x) $ over the density $ p(x) $. After sampling a large number of random variables, $ (x_{1},\\cdots,x_{n}) $, from the density $ p(x) $, we have that $$ \\int_{a}^{b}h(x)dx=E_{p(x)}[f(x)]\\simeq\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(x_{i}) $$",
      "source_page": 1209,
      "source_pages": [
        1209
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix3:p002:b5",
        "Evolution_appendix3:p002:b6",
        "Evolution_appendix3:p002:b8",
        "Evolution_appendix3:p002:b9"
      ],
